The 7 Words of Jesus and their meaning
In general, we tend to take into account the last words of people who are about to die, thereby the 7 words of Jesus said in the crucifixion process are of no less importance. In this article we will go into detail about these words and their meaning.
Importance of the last 7 words of Jesus
Let us suppose a person who was, due to some crime committed, sentenced to death by means of the law: this person, before executing him, is taken into account his last words, and sometimes his wishes. Under this premise, why not take into account the 7 words of Jesus when he was crucified and during his process?
The words are taken from the canonical Gospel books; stories from the books of Mark, Matthew, John and Luke, which among the four collect the phrases that Jesus said.
Importance and meditation on the 7 words of Jesus
In particular, these famous and highly devoted phrases are revered by Christians, as they consider that they were the true words of Jesus.
Luke 23:24. "Father, forgive them because they dont know what they are doing."
If you found this post interesting, we invite you to read our article on:Passion, death and resurrection of Jesus.
And we with good reason, because we have deserved it with our actions; instead, this one has done nothing wrong. Jesus, remember me when you come with your Kingdom ”.
The context of the phrase is that when Jesus was on the cross, there were his mother, his mother's sister, and his beloved disciple. When Jesus knew the fate that awaited him, he left his beloved disciple as a son to his mother.
In this way, Jesus before dying entrusted him to his beloved disciple, his mother; being now this mother of his beloved disciple. Jesus said the above phrase to both of them, and he welcomed her into his home.
Shortly before Jesus died, on the cross, he loudly exclaimed to heaven "Elí, Elí, lamá sabactaní?"
However, Jesus accepted his work, being sacrificed to cleanse the sin of the world, although his suffering is also a reflection of the human feeling towards suffering.
Likewise, in a metaphorical sense, it can be understood that by "thirst" he meant those eagerness to fulfill his spiritual work, having finally accomplished the redemption for all humanity.
Jesus already knew that he had achieved his task, fulfilling what was established in the Holy Scriptures, thereby satisfying the will of his Father. Jesus was given vinegar to drink when he said the fifth sentence, and when he drank it, he ended up saying "Everything is finished."
Before dying, Jesus already knew that his work was accomplished with his sacrifice, with which he said his last sentence with a cry to heaven "Father, in your hands I put my spirit! ", And immediately he vanished.
