In different situations the last words of a person are considered important, but in particular when it is known or presumed that the moment of his death will come, which is why they will be his last words.
Well, these are called last 7 words of Jesus, because they refer to the last sentences that Jesus said before his death on the cross, during the process of his sacrifice.
However, and before beginning to detail which were the phrases of Jesus, we must clarify that they do not have an exact chronological order; they just follow the traditional order.
In this section of our article we want to go into the details of these sentences, because they have a historical context that should not go unnoticed, since it will help to better understand the reason for the sentences; for example, in the cases of the second, sixth, first, and so on.
When Jesus was crucified with the criminals, he said the phrase on the air, which may lead to believe that it was directed towards the Roman soldiers; or towards the Jews; it could have been towards both; It might even be believed that Jesus said that phrase towards humanity.
"I assure you that today you will be with me in paradise." The context of the word of Jesus It comes from a discussion that the three condemned had, where one of them shouted to Jesus “Are you not the Christ?
"Woman, there you have your son ... Son, there you have your mother."
In principle, this happens because Jesus had the responsibility of taking care of his mother, who is believed or supposed to be a widow and had only one begotten, Jesus.
Why have you abandoned me?"
This phrase is a reflection of his human nature, where he feels abandoned by God; just as it happened in the garden of Gethsemane.
In this phrase there are possibly two meanings: in principle, the physiological thirst, dehydration due to the suffering and martyrdom suffered by those sentenced to death by crucifixion.
A victory phrase, even if it doesn't seem like it. Jesus already knew very well what his job was: to be the savior and redeemer of the men and women of the world, cleansing the sins of humanity before his father.
"Father, in your hands I put my spirit!"
