For this reason, there are now many games and exercises that make it easy to memorize the results of the multiplication table.
Starting with the simplest multiplication tables, for example, 2, 5 and 10, can be a good way to learn to memorize the multiplication tables.
Now we will learn to build the Cartesian multiplication table. First draw a large square with 11 rows and 11 columns.
At this point, our multiplication table will look like the following figure:
In the third column we will fill in with the multiplication table of 2. For this, you can add the two numbers that are written on the same line, as shown in the figure:
We note that 4 is equal to 2 × 2. Therefore, we can write in the column of the multiplication table 4 the result of the values of the multiplication table 2 multiplied by 2.
We observe that 6 is equal to 2 × 3, so we are going to place the result of the values of the table of 3 times multiplied by 2 in the column that refers to the table of 6 times, as shown in figure a continuation.
Finally, we complete the table with the multiplication table of 10. To do this, simply put the numbers from 1 to 10 in the last column and then put 0 at the end of each one.
For example, if we want to know what 7 x 9 is, just follow the column of the number 7 with the line of the number 9, where they meet is the result of the multiplication.
It is worth remembering that by adding and subtracting numbers, we can better memorize and understand the relationship between them.
The multiplication table is a system used in mathematics that gathers the multiples and divisors of numbers in an organized way.
The multiplication table is also called Pythagoras Times Table, in honor of the Greek mathematician and philosopher Pythagoras.
