Neither provider provides detailed coverage maps specifically for their 5G home internet solutions.They’re also not yet included in the FCC’s database of broadband providers.But given the total number of cities they claim to cover, T-Mobile is clearly in the lead.
While 5G is capable of delivering gigabit download speeds, don’t expect these high-end numbers from either provider’s home internet offerings.For example, T-Mobile states on its website that customers can expect to see “average download speeds in excess of 100Mbps” and experience a typical range of 35-115Mbps.Why so low?To speed up its availability, T-Mobile Home Internet relies on its growing 5G mesh and existing 4G LTE network.So don’t expect a pure 5G experience.
Verizon, which averages 300Mbps, halved that figure, costing just under 24 cents per Mbps.Plus, if you opt for the Verizon 5G Plus plan, your monthly fee will drop to $50 per month, and the cost per Mbps will drop to under 17 cents.This is quite affordable compared to existing cable and fiber internet plans.
Still not convinced by the straightforward terms that T-Mobile and Verizon are offering?They’ll try to influence you with their promotional offers — although Verizon clearly has the edge here.
Both T-Mobile and Verizon are actively building out their 5G networks, so we’re much closer to the beginning of the story than the middle or the end, especially as it relates to 5G fixed wireless internet in general.
