News - 16 Tips to Help Remote Workers Solve Tech Problems (and Avoid Them in the First Place)
16 Tips to Help Remote Workers Solve Tech Problems (and Avoid Them in the First Place)
When working in an office, it’s easy to get into the habit of automatically pinging IT when your computer or software isn’t working as expected.Technical team members are always willing to help, but remote workers can do a good job of learning some troubleshooting basics – you’ll get back faster if you can diagnose and correct the problem yourself, and sometimes fixes can only be done by on-site personnel.
Write down all the things you think could be wrong.List the most likely problems first.Then, step through the list and test each possible problem until you find the problem.It sounds obvious, but most people don’t solve problems logically and methodically and are only mentally overwhelmed.- Adam Ayers, No. 5
Make sure you are running the latest version of the software, including any updates.Software is developed according to the concepts of continuous integration, continuous delivery (CI/CD) and rapid change.A common mistake is not keeping the software up to date, which can lead to communication failures, corruption, or security issues.- Geeman Yip, BitTitan
My advice is to be concise and clear when reporting your issue.Too often, end users fail to provide the necessary information that IT specialists need to diagnose the problem.Get in the habit of providing OS, software, and browser type/version.Take screenshots or screenshots to show an expert how to reproduce the problem.Just saying “my internet doesn’t work” isn’t enough information.- Junior Bernadin, Ron Clark College
A meta tip for anyone dealing with remote work tech is finding the right people willing to help.If Slack is your company’s most-used community tool, make sure everyone is proficient in Slack and knows how to search for channels.Learn about the “IT Room,” the “Zoom Tips Room,” or the “Mac Hangout” – channels you might need when dealing with issues.There might even be a “Slack Help Channel” (get metadata about it).-Eve Maler, ForgeRock
Life is busy and many of us are doing a thousand things at the same time.We have many applications running on our computers and many tabs open on our web browsers.This slows down the computer and sometimes we don’t restart our laptop for weeks on end.You’ll be surprised how many “phantom” problems go away by simply restarting the system before calling IT.-Mercedes Soria, Knight Mirror
Think of your home office as a mission-critical business network node.Make sure the cables have labels indicating the source and destination.Take simple, proactive steps to ensure redundancy, starting with power and network (internet) failures, all the way to having backup headsets when needed.Remember, the internet itself has tons of easy-to-search how-to solutions, often with videos.- Philip Samson, Ericsson
Common tech issues include poor AV quality during virtual meetings or unreliable home Wi-Fi, so relatively small investments like headphones or range extenders can help.Even if you don’t have to buy a new device, there are ways to enhance your home Wi-Fi, such as updating your router’s firmware or simply relocating the physical router to a more open space in your home.- Venkat Viswanathan, LatentView Analysis
Beyond turning your device off and on again, it’s best to develop some perspective around the wonders of modern technology.Yes, Zoom will drop, Wi-Fi will slow down, and you may have to restart your computer, re-read the manual, and flash your router.Take a few extra minutes in your meeting and try not to take it too seriously.Others have the same technical problem.—Rich Waldron, Tray.io
