Wireless providers will turn off 3G throughout the year, leaving an undisclosed number of devices out of service.
The expansion of 5G is good news for the growing number of people with 5G devices, and it could be a key step toward more advanced technologies, such as self-driving cars and virtual reality.But turning off 3G will also disable an entire generation of technology: everything from 3G cell phones to car crash notification systems.For those who rely on these devices, the shift will cut off the cellular networks they have relied on for years and, in some cases, critical security equipment.
At the same time, 3G device owners need to prepare for the impending 3G shutdown.Some may not even know that their service is about to go offline.Depending on their provider, these customers may only have weeks or months to upgrade their technology.At the moment, it is unclear whether they will be able to make the switch in time.
Beyond the logistics of how radio spectrum works, wireless providers are also reallocating 3G spectrum because it makes more economic sense for them.Verizon and AT&T estimate that less than 1 percent of services are still running on 3G networks, while 90 million 5G devices shipped last year alone.Once 3G finally shuts down, wireless providers can devote more resources to expanding their 5G networks and convincing customers to upgrade their service plans.
That’s why some people think 3G should be online for a while longer.AARP said the pandemic has prevented many seniors from updating their technology and wants to delay the closure until the end of the year.Alarm companies, including those that make fire and carbon monoxide detectors and home security systems, have also asked for an extension.They say a shortage of computer chips has hindered their efforts to produce and install replacement equipment.
History inevitably shows that cellular networks come and go.The next cellular network, 6G, may be less than 10 years away, and it could be used to introduce everything from 3D holograms to connected clothing.By then, 5G seems less fresh and exciting, and the days of 4G may be over.
