opens IMAGE file “So, what would you like to talk about today?” asked Caroline, my college career counselor, as I walked into her office one morning at the beginning of my senior year.
I came to her office because I wanted to start planning my job search, but I didn’t even know what kind of job I wanted to look for.
I took this advice with me to the UMass nonprofit and social enterprise career fair and went over to the organization that looked the most interesting; the Center for EcoTechnology. Over the past few months, I had begun to garner that I was interested in working in sustainability in some fashion and after speaking to the EcoFellows at the table, I knew I had found a good opportunity.
Additionally, I am responsible for creating and presenting webinars. My fellowship started a few months after we stopped all of our usual in-person outreach events due to the pandemic, and my co-EcoFellow Ozette and I have been tasked with moving all of our outreach online.
I’ve made a lot of graphics for our website and social media like this Instagram tile promoting our energy efficiency month events
One of my favorite parts of the program has been creating and working on my own projects. It’s really gratifying to be trusted with such big and impactful projects so early on.
I like to start working at 8 so I can take more time for lunch, and my early morning usually consists of creative projects. I usually use this time slot to write or edit blog posts, do research for webinars, write ads and captions for social media, create our weekly e-blast newsletter, or work on a slide deck.
We also occasionally have introduction meetings with board members on CET’s Board of Directors and EcoFellowship alumni, and we’ll also have the occasional training with a different team within our organization.
I’ve enjoyed it more than I thought I would. It’s obviously a bit of a bummer that I haven’t had the opportunity to meet my colleagues in person, and that I’ve missed out on events and random chit chat with people throughout the day but I think CET as a whole has adjusted to the situation as well as they could have.
Our organization is also very interconnected through email, Slack, and Microsoft Teams so it’s always easy to reach out to anyone with any questions you might have. I’m also impressed by how CET’s friendly and welcoming attitude has shined through despite the remote environment.
What skills have you gained during your fellowship?
Also, as I mentioned before, I’ve gained really crucial organization and project management skills through my varied work responsibilities and long-term projects. I’ve also been able to build my sales and community building skills working with our outreach program.
There are many things that make this experience unique, but my three favorites are the emphasis on professional development, our ability to lead our own projects, and the EcoFellow network.
One of the things I love about the fellowship is that this attitude of searching is not only accepted but encouraged. We are encouraged to seek out new opportunities within the organization that interest us, and we can take paid work time every once in a while for professional development.
Although I’ve never met her in person, I’ve really enjoyed working with Ozette and meeting some of the Ecofellowship alumni!
I’ve also really enjoyed the opportunity to lead my own projects. Most entry level positions don’t offer the opportunity to suggest and implement an idea within your first few months on the job, but I’ve already had the opportunity to do so.
