Have you ever brought home fresh produce, placed it in your fridge, only to forget about it until a week later, and then, like an archaeologist discovering an ancient artifact, you dig it out and notice something fuzzy growing on it? We’ve all been there.
One key driver of consumer food waste is the misconception with date labeling. Roughly 20% of household food waste comes from the confusion over the dates stamped on the food packaging opens PDF file .
In fact, these “best if used by” claims only relate to the quality of the product. These labels are not necessarily saying that this food isn’t safe to eat; they’re simply the manufacturer’s suggestion for the item’s peak quality.
Properly storing food is a great way to extend the “life” of a food item and can keep food fresh for longer.
Avoid buying products with a limited shelf life in bulk, like dairy or produce
